WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
AI workloads rarely live in one neat place.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.340
A team may prototype on developer machines, test on lab hardware,

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:09.820
run batch jobs in a private cluster, serve inference from Kubernetes,

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:12.920
use managed cloud services for storage or model endpoints,

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.000
and deploy selected workloads at the edge.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.465
A hybrid AI environment combines these locations, ownership models,

00:00:18.465 --> 00:00:19.880
and operational assumptions.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:23.725
A single user request may touch an API gateway, authentication service,

00:00:23.725 --> 00:00:28.505
model-serving route, retrieval service, vector store, cache, database, queue, GPU node,

00:00:28.505 --> 00:00:29.640
and external service.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.110
A failure in any one layer can look like a model problem from the outside.

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:34.120
Without enough context,

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:38.395
teams may spend valuable time debating whether the issue belongs to the application,

00:00:38.395 --> 00:00:41.915
infrastructure, data platform, security controls, or a provider dependency.

00:00:41.915 --> 00:00:43.651
Clusters add another dimension.

00:00:43.651 --> 00:00:47.020
Scheduling, resource allocation, workload placement, node health,

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.040
accelerator availability, queue depth,

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.730
and noisy-neighbor effects all influence AI behavior.

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:52.840
A model may be correct,

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:56.510
but it can still miss latency expectations if it waits too long for a GPU,

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:00.720
loads from slow storage, or shares a node with a workload that consumes memory bandwidth.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.240
Hybrid systems also introduce network boundaries, identity boundaries, data movement,

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:06.914
and operational ownership questions.

00:01:06.914 --> 00:01:10.320
Telemetry should make these layers visible enough for teams to act together.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:11.920
The goal is not to collect everything.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.720
The goal is to collect the signals that reveal where work traveled,

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.135
what resources it used, which boundary it crossed,

00:01:17.135 --> 00:01:19.520
and what changed when behavior became unhealthy.
